WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.203
A square matrix of full rank can be undone.

2
00:00:06.203 --> 00:00:09.507
This one turns the circle into an ellipse

3
00:00:09.507 --> 00:00:12.580
and its inverse turns it straight back.

4
00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.234
Now take this three by two matrix B

5
00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:17.427
of rank one.

6
00:00:17.519 --> 00:00:19.813
B times x only depends on

7
00:00:19.905 --> 00:00:21.833
x one plus two x two.

8
00:00:21.924 --> 00:00:25.228
Every input on one of these parallel

9
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.890
lines gives the same output.

10
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.805
So can anything undo B?

11
00:00:30.805 --> 00:00:35.265
And if not, what is the best we can do?

12
00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:37.160
Pause and think.

13
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.997
Start with a case that goes half way.

14
00:00:43.997 --> 00:00:47.079
Three readings, one, two and four

15
00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:49.544
at times zero, one and two.

16
00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:53.242
A line c plus d t through all three would

17
00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.587
need three equations in two unknowns

18
00:00:56.587 --> 00:00:58.260
no exact solution.

19
00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:00.717
The matrix A is tall

20
00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:03.325
with independent columns.

21
00:01:03.325 --> 00:01:07.439
Then A transpose A is invertible, and L

22
00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:10.449
the inverse of A transpose A

23
00:01:10.449 --> 00:01:14.261
times A transpose, is a left inverse

24
00:01:14.261 --> 00:01:16.970
L times A is the identity.

25
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.254
Applied to the readings

26
00:01:19.335 --> 00:01:21.592
L gives c equals five sixths

27
00:01:21.672 --> 00:01:23.768
and d equals three halves

28
00:01:23.768 --> 00:01:25.702
the least squares line.

29
00:01:25.702 --> 00:01:28.362
It misses each reading a little

30
00:01:28.442 --> 00:01:31.021
but its total squared miss is as

31
00:01:31.102 --> 00:01:33.520
small as any line can make it.

32
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.702
So L undoes A.

33
00:01:35.702 --> 00:01:38.333
Does A undo L as well?

34
00:01:38.448 --> 00:01:41.308
It is tempting to think A

35
00:01:41.423 --> 00:01:43.940
L is the identity too.

36
00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.620
But A times L is three by three

37
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:47.930
and has rank two.

38
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.532
Feed it the readings

39
00:01:49.532 --> 00:01:52.444
and it returns the heights of the line

40
00:01:52.444 --> 00:01:54.192
zero point eight three

41
00:01:54.192 --> 00:01:55.866
two point three three

42
00:01:55.866 --> 00:01:57.686
three point eight three.

43
00:01:57.686 --> 00:02:00.890
The readings themselves are not given back.

44
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.347
The product A times L is the projection

45
00:02:04.426 --> 00:02:06.179
onto the column space

46
00:02:06.179 --> 00:02:09.047
it keeps what the line can produce

47
00:02:09.047 --> 00:02:10.640
and drops the rest.

48
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.240
A wide matrix has the mirror image.

49
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.261
Take one equation in two unknowns

50
00:02:17.261 --> 00:02:19.073
x plus y equals two.

51
00:02:19.073 --> 00:02:22.180
Every point on this line solves it.

52
00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.681
The row is one, one

53
00:02:24.789 --> 00:02:29.091
and the right inverse R is its transpose

54
00:02:29.198 --> 00:02:32.963
times the inverse of W W transpose

55
00:02:32.963 --> 00:02:35.114
one half, one half.

56
00:02:35.114 --> 00:02:37.050
W times R is one.

57
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.419
R times two is the point one, one

58
00:02:40.419 --> 00:02:41.573
on the line.

59
00:02:41.573 --> 00:02:43.082
So is two, zero

60
00:02:43.082 --> 00:02:45.657
and every other point of it.

61
00:02:45.745 --> 00:02:47.876
R picks out one solution

62
00:02:47.965 --> 00:02:49.918
among infinitely many

63
00:02:49.918 --> 00:02:51.960
the one along the row.

64
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:55.808
Now every matrix, even B.

65
00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:59.992
Write A as U Sigma V transpose.

66
00:02:59.992 --> 00:03:04.306
A sends each v to sigma times u.

67
00:03:04.437 --> 00:03:08.359
The pseudoinverse sends each u

68
00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.020
back to v divided by sigma.

69
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.469
Sigma plus inverts only the singular

70
00:03:15.555 --> 00:03:17.707
values that are not zero.

71
00:03:17.707 --> 00:03:19.601
What A sends to zero

72
00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:21.667
nothing can bring back.

73
00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:25.110
In pieces, A plus is the sum of v times

74
00:03:25.196 --> 00:03:28.640
u transpose over sigma: the pieces of A

75
00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.050
turned around and inverted.

76
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.212
B has a single singular value

77
00:03:34.212 --> 00:03:37.119
the square root of forty five.

78
00:03:37.119 --> 00:03:40.025
Its v one runs along one, two

79
00:03:40.025 --> 00:03:41.431
the row space.

80
00:03:41.525 --> 00:03:43.775
So B plus is B transpose

81
00:03:43.869 --> 00:03:45.931
divided by forty five

82
00:03:45.931 --> 00:03:49.400
and it only ever lands on this line.

83
00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.193
So is B plus times B the identity?

84
00:03:53.193 --> 00:03:53.571
No.

85
00:03:53.571 --> 00:03:57.445
It is the projection onto the row space.

86
00:03:57.445 --> 00:03:59.901
Take the input two, zero.

87
00:03:59.996 --> 00:04:03.114
It drops along the null direction

88
00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:06.799
onto the row line, at zero point four

89
00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:08.500
zero point eight.

90
00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.622
Now take zero, one.

91
00:04:10.622 --> 00:04:13.978
It lands on exactly the same point.

92
00:04:13.978 --> 00:04:17.614
B sent both inputs to the same output

93
00:04:17.614 --> 00:04:21.810
so no matrix can tell them apart afterwards.

94
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.583
Project the whole unit circle

95
00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:27.007
and it collapses onto a short

96
00:04:27.088 --> 00:04:28.865
piece of the row line.

97
00:04:28.865 --> 00:04:32.338
Undoing is possible only on the row space

98
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:34.600
the null direction is lost.

99
00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.738
When A has independent columns

100
00:04:37.738 --> 00:04:40.956
its pseudoinverse is the left inverse.

101
00:04:40.956 --> 00:04:43.432
When it has independent rows

102
00:04:43.432 --> 00:04:45.495
it is the right inverse.

103
00:04:45.495 --> 00:04:48.135
And it exists for every matrix

104
00:04:48.135 --> 00:04:49.786
built from the SVD.

105
00:04:49.786 --> 00:04:53.416
A A plus and A plus A are both projections

106
00:04:53.416 --> 00:04:56.057
and with two more simple rules

107
00:04:56.057 --> 00:04:58.780
they pin down A plus completely.

108
00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.229
Next part

109
00:05:00.229 --> 00:05:04.513
why A plus times b is the best answer

110
00:05:04.513 --> 00:05:06.930
and the shortest one.
